Let $n \in \Z_{>0}$ be a (strictly) positive integer.
Let $p$ be a prime factor of $n!$ with multiplicity $\mu$.
Let $n$ be expressed in a base $p$ representation as:
{{begin-eqn}}
{{eqn | l = n
      | r = \sum_{j \mathop = 0}^m a_j p^j
      | c = where $0 \le a_j < p$
}}
{{eqn | r = a_0 + a_1 p + a_2 p^2 + \cdots + a_m p^m
      | c = for some $m > 0$
}}
{{end-eqn}}
Then:
:$\dfrac {n!} {p^\mu} \equiv \paren {-1}^\mu a_0! a_1! \dotsb a_m! \pmod p$

Let $ABCD$ be a parallelogram. The interior angle bisector of $\angle ADC$ intersects the line $BC$ at $E$, and the perpendicular bisector of the side $AD$ intersects the line $DE$ at $M$. Let $F = AM \cap BC$. Prove that:

a) $DE = AF$;  
b) $AD \cdot AB = DE \cdot DM$.

Let $ P(x) \in \mathbb{Z}[x] $ with $ \deg P = n $. Prove that for all $ n \geq 1 $, there exists $ x \in \mathbb{N} $ such that
$$
\left\{
\begin{aligned}
P(x) &= k_1 k_2 \cdots k_n, \\
\gcd(k_i, k_j) &= 1 \quad \text{for all } i \ne j.
\end{aligned}
\right.
$$

Let $\triangle ABC$ be a triangle with incenter $I$ and orthocenter $H$. Denote $D \in AI \cap BC$ and $P \in AH \cap BC$. Prove the following relations:
$$
\left\{
\begin{array}{cc}
(1)\ : & \dfrac{\sqrt{b(s-b)}}{IB} + \dfrac{\sqrt{c(s-c)}}{IC} = \dfrac{\sqrt{a(s-a)}}{ID} \\
(2)\ : & \dfrac{c}{HC} + \dfrac{b}{HB} = \dfrac{a}{HP}
\end{array}
\right.
$$

